// ---- 0x3CC (972) : CHASSIS FAULT LATCH (non-E2E, ~0.6 Hz) ------------------
BO_ 972 FAULT_LATCH_3CC: 8 Vector__XXX
 SG_ fault_latch    : 7|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
 SG_ config_gen_3cc : 23|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
// [STRONG] D1 (fault_latch) = 0x7D in ALL normal states (incl. a TPMS fault), = 0x7E ONLY in
//   the IM brownout fault + ride1 TC-slip (D2 tracks: 09 normal / 39 fault). A stored fault /
//   abnormal-dynamics latch. D3 (config_gen_3cc) = slow per-session config-gen counter
//   (19->1A->1B). D4=FF,D5=FE,D6=FE sentinel tail. *** REFINES notes' "config/version head,
//   rarely updated" - the head is a STATE-LINKED fault latch. ***
// [OBSERVATION TO RECONCILE 2026-07-29 r1 — do NOT flip the decode] Across the ENTIRE streamed
//   corpus (s6..s58, all 10 sessions: park / city / freeway / charge) D1 reads 0x7E and D2 reads
//   0x39 CONTINUOUSLY - i.e. the very values this decode calls the "fault" state, in every regime
//   including calm parked baselines. D3 = the config-gen counter (0x1B->0x1C here); the D4/D5/D6
//   tail is unchanged. TWO possibilities, unresolved: (a) this specific bike carries a STANDING
//   stored-fault / abnormal-dynamics latch that never clears, OR (b) the "0x7E only-in-fault /
//   0x7D normal, 0x39 fault / 0x09 normal" assignment (derived from the legacy laptop captures) is
//   simply WRONG for this firmware/config and 0x7E/0x39 is the NORMAL idle state here. Not enough
//   evidence to choose - leaving the decode as-is and flagging the discrepancy for a future
//   known-good-state read.


// ---- 0x15A (346) : see FRONT_BRAKE_HAZARD_15A above (hazards merged there) ---


// ---- 0x160 (352) : SPEED (primary vehicle speed) + second speed/RPM ---------
BO_ 352 SPEED_160: 8 IM
 SG_ vehicle_speed : 7|16@0+ (0.1,0) [0|6553.5] "km/h" Vector__XXX
 SG_ motor_rpm     : 23|16@0+ (1,-1000) [-1000|64535] "rpm" Vector__XXX
 SG_ cruise_setpoint : 39|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "km/h" Vector__XXX
 SG_ cruise_armed : 47|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
// [CONFIRMED] 0x160 D1:D2 (big-endian) = vehicle speed, unit km/h x10.
//   UNSIGNED: reads POSITIVE for both forward AND reverse motion (confirmed by
//   manual forward+backward spin - backward still showed positive D1:D2). For
//   direction, use D3:D4 (signed, offset 1000) instead.
//   value/10 = km/h ; (value/10)/1.60934 = mph.
//   Cross-checks: 10mph run -> 161 (=16.1km/h=10.0mph);
//                 5mph manual spin -> ~80; 75mph run peak D1:D2=1248 (=77.6mph).
//   Confirmed on the UNPOWERED manual-spin capture too => it is genuine wheel/
//   vehicle speed, independent of motor power.
// [CONFIRMED] 0x160 D3:D4 (big-endian) = MOTOR RPM + 1000 offset.
//   motor_rpm = (D3:D4) - 1000.  Positive = forward rotation, negative = reverse
//   (backward hand-spin drove D3:D4 below 1000). Signed via the offset.
//   DERIVATION (closes exactly): D3:D4 scales at 28.94 counts per km/h (regression
//   vs calibrated D1:D2 speed, r=0.998). Drivetrain from spec: motor -1:1 primary->
//   84:22 (3.818) belt -> wheel; 150/70-19 DT1 tire OD ~0.70m (circ 2.199m).
//   That gives 28.94 motor-RPM per km/h => 1.000 count per motor RPM. Exact.
//   At 103mph top speed: ~4800 motor RPM => D3:D4 ~5800. This is a true TACH.
//   Also cross-validates tire circumference ~0.70m (consistent with odometer).
//   Verified reverse via manual_spin_forward_back: forward drove D3:D4 above 1000,
//   backward below 1000, symmetric.
//   [UDS CROSS-WITNESS 2026-07-30, ultracode] VSC UDS DID 020C word0 (offset-binary 0x8000,
//     1 rpm/ct) is the point-to-point twin of this D3:D4 tach and tracks it 1:1 in reverse
//     (s8: 020C -32/-50/-90 vs 0x160 -32/-48/-88). Direction on the whole bike is recoverable
//     ONLY from this signed motor-rpm (broadcast 0x160 D3:D4 or UDS 020C) - NO drive-state
//     enum, reverse bit, or reverse-throttle level exists on secondary (see 0x161 block + a
//     66-file corpus scan). Reverse is a SUB-STATE of enabled DRIVE, sign-differentiated only.
// [CONFIRMED] 0x160 D5 = CRUISE SET-POINT SPEED, in km/h (integer), 0 when not
//   engaged. This is the ENGAGED/SET state, distinct from merely armed.
//   PROOF OF ISOLATION: D5 = 0x00 in ALL 34 other captures - including the
//   cruise-ARMED-but-never-SET capture (cruisecontrol_3x_onoff). It only becomes
//   non-zero once cruise is actually SET. Perfect armed-vs-engaged separation.
//   UNITS: correlated against the calibrated speed (D1:D2) during stable holds:
//       D5=16 -> measured 17.4 km/h
//       D5=18 -> measured 18.9 km/h
//       D5=21 -> measured 20.7 km/h
//   i.e. D5 tracks the held speed in km/h within ~1 km/h. (Two other D5 values,
//   19 and 22, show contaminated medians because they span the ramp-up and the
//   fault/disengage, not steady cruising.)
//   Rider set points were nominally 11/12/13/14/15 mph on the dash; the bike
//   stores the set point metric, consistent with everything else on this bus.
//   *** MODEL RESOLVED *** by a narrated fresh-power-cycle capture
//   (cruisecontrol_set_unsetbybutton_set_unsetbybrake). CRUISE STATE IS THE PAIR
//   (D6 bit7, D5 setpoint), and bit7 means "NOT CURRENTLY CONTROLLING" - it is SET
//   while standing by and CLEAR when either off or actively engaged:
//        bit7=0, sp=0   -> OFF
//        bit7=1, sp=0   -> ARMED (cruise on, no target yet)
//        bit7=0, sp>0   -> ENGAGED, actively holding sp km/h
//        bit7=1, sp>0   -> CANCELLED with target RETAINED (resume available)
//   OBSERVED SEQUENCE (percent of that capture):
//        22.8%  bit7 0->1, sp 0       ARM
//        31.9%  bit7 1->0, sp 0->20   SET / engage at 20 km/h
//        42.1%  bit7 0,    sp 20->0   CANCEL BY BUTTON - setpoint CLEARED
//        58.0%  bit7 0->1, sp 0       ARM again
//        64.7%  bit7 1->0, sp 0->17   SET / engage at 17 km/h
//        76.3%  bit7 0->1, sp stays 17  CANCEL BY BRAKE - setpoint RETAINED
//   *** THE TWO CANCEL METHODS DIFFER ***: the button clears the stored target,
//   the brake keeps it for resume. That is standard cruise behaviour and it also
//   EXPLAINS the earlier apparent contradiction - the cruisecontrol_92f capture
//   simply started mid-session with a setpoint left over from a previous brake
//   cancel, so D5 was non-zero before bit7 ever set.
// [CONFIRMED] 0x160 D6 bit7 (0x80) = CRUISE CONTROL ARMED (enabled, not engaged).
//   0x09/0x05 base -> 0x89/0x85 when cruise is switched ON.
//   Isolated with cruisecontrol_3x_onoff: exactly 3 contiguous pulses matching the
//   3 arm/hold/disarm cycles, and bit7 is set in 50% of that capture's frames.
//   CROSS-CHECKED AGAINST ALL 34 OTHER CAPTURES: bit7 is set in 0% of every single
//   one. No false positives anywhere. This is arm/enable state only - the SET/
//   engage state (actively holding a speed) is a separate, still-unmapped signal.
//   Other bits of D6 (0,2,3) vary independently (base values 0x05 vs 0x09).
// D7=rolling counter(0-63), D8=checksum (ignore).
// NOTE: 0x160 D4 also carried a small high-beam-linked change in one capture;
//   treat D3:D4 decode as [STRONG] not [CONFIRMED].


// ---- 0x162 (354) : energy-flow direction / mode + kickstand -----------------
BO_ 354 DRIVETRAIN_162: 8 Vector__XXX
 SG_ energy_direction : 31|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
 SG_ start_inhibit    : 39|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
 SG_ kickstand_162    : 47|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
// VAL_ 0xC8=down 0xA8=up 0x28=up+motor_ready
// [CONFIRMED] 0x162 D4 = NIBBLE-PACKED drive state + energy flow:
//   HIGH nibble = drive-enable state:  0x4_ = MOTOR ENABLED,  0x8_ = NOT enabled.
//     Verified across the full sequence: 0x81 while ign-on-but-motor-off, during
//     ENABLING, during DISABLING and when off; 0x41 for the entire motor-enabled
//     period (ready, rolling, braking).
//   LOW nibble = energy-flow / load detail (0x_1 rest, 0x_2 driving, 0x_6 regen
//     seen in earlier captures). Treat low nibble as [STRONG], high nibble as
//     [CONFIRMED].
// [CONFIRMED] 0x162 D5 = START-INHIBIT BITFIELD (each bit = one reason the bike
//   will refuse to enable the motor; the cluster reads these to choose which
//   message to display, e.g. "UNLOCK FORK LOCK"):
//     bit1 (0x02) = KICKSTAND DOWN.  Verified against the independent kickstand
//        byte (D6, 0xC8=down / 0xA8=up): 100 % agreement in 43 of 44 captures.
//        The only exception is test.csv (73 %), which is the boot capture full of
//        0xFF invalid values - i.e. not a real disagreement.
//     bit2 (0x04) = FORK LOCK ENGAGED (see below).
//   *** BITFIELD MODEL CONFIRMED *** A dedicated capture stepping through all four
//   combinations produced exactly: 0x00 (neither), 0x02 (kickstand only),
//   0x04 (fork lock only), and 0x06 (BOTH asserted simultaneously). Independent
//   reason bits, not a priority-encoded enum. This retroactively validates reading
//   the multi-bit values (0x0A, 0x22, 0x28, 0x2A, 0xA8, 0xAA) as several inhibits
//   asserted at once.
//   DISPLAY vs BUS: the rider reports the dash always shows the FORK LOCK message
//   when both apply. So the BUS carries the complete state (both bits) while the
//   CLUSTER applies precedence to choose one message. Do not infer bus state from
//   the dash text - the dash is lossy by design.
//
//     bit0 (0x01) = THROTTLE NOT AT ZERO  [STRONG]. Set for the whole period the
//        rider held the throttle open (17-72 % of throttle_inhibit.csv, 54 % of
//        frames) and essentially absent from all 45 other captures. A START press
//        during that window was refused (0x133 D1: 0x60 -> 0x80 -> 0x60), so it
//        behaves as an inhibit. Slightly weaker than the kickstand/fork evidence
//        because it was one continuous hold rather than 3 separate pulses.
//   NOTE: THROTTLE POSITION ITSELF IS NOT ON SECONDARY. No byte anywhere differed
//     between throttle-held and throttle-closed. This is expected: per the EDM the
//     Twist Grip Sensor [204]/[224] wires into the RHCM, and the RHCM is a PRIMARY
//     bus module. Only the binary "throttle not zero" inhibit is gatewayed here.
//   BITS OBSERVED IN USE BUT NOT YET IDENTIFIED: bit3 (0x08),
//     bit5 (0x20), bit7 (0x80). Bits 3/5/7 only appear during ignition and boot
//     captures, so they are plausibly "system not ready" inhibits that clear once
//     initialisation completes. Observed combined values: 0x0A, 0x22, 0x28, 0x2A,
//     0xA8, 0xA9, 0xAA - i.e. several inhibits can be asserted simultaneously.
//   *** CHARGING INHIBIT: NO BIT IS SET, AND NO START IS ATTEMPTED ***
//     A capture with the bike CHARGING and START pressed shows 0x162 D5 = 0x00 for
//     the entire capture AND 0x133 D1 with ZERO transitions (stays 0x50 CHARGING).
//     This is a DIFFERENT refusal mechanism from fork lock / kickstand / throttle:
//       - those set an inhibit bit AND show an attempted enable (0x60->0x80->0x60)
//       - charging shows neither: the START press is ignored outright, the state
//         machine never leaves 0x50.
//     So "cannot start while charging" is enforced by the state machine refusing to
//     leave the CHARGING state, not by the inhibit bitfield. Do not expect a
//     charging bit in 0x162 D5.
//   (earlier note) a 1.1 kW AC charge capture did NOT clearly set a new inhibit bit -
//     0x162 D5 read 0x00 for almost the whole capture, with single stray frames of
//     0x10 and 0x18 (bit4 / bit3|bit4). bit4 (0x10) had never been seen before, so
//     it MAY be charge-related, but one frame is not evidence. NOTE the capture did
//     not include a START press while plugged in, so the charging inhibit was never
//     actually exercised. REDO: plug in, then press START 3x and watch D5.
//   CHARGE POWER: still UNCONFIRMED. With two known levels (1.1 kW and 1.4 kW,
//     ratio 1.273) the best byte-aligned candidate is 0x1A7 D4: 168 -> 216
//     (ratio 1.286). Plausible but NOT confirmed - two points cannot separate scale
//     from offset, and no clean unit fits (implies ~160 counts/kW, or ~18.5/A at
//     120 V AC, or ~61/A on a 400 V pack). Other candidates (0x164 D3, 0x181 D2)
//     are worse fits and 0x164 is a known multiplexed frame.
//     NEEDED: a THIRD charge power, ideally very different (Level 2 at ~5 kW) to
//     pin the scale. Same lesson as SoC, which needed three points.
//   (earlier note) not found in the first capture: charge power/current. The only fields reading
//     11 (=1.1 kW x10) were 0x3C4 D2 (a known rolling counter) and a noisy 0x126
//     byte - neither survives scrutiny. SoC stayed at 97 % throughout, so the
//     capture was likely too short for charge accumulation to show.
//   REMAINING INHIBITS TO IDENTIFY: bits 3, 5, 7 (ignition/boot only), plus
//     whatever covers charging. Method: create the condition,
//     press START three times, and see which bit is set during the refusals.
//   REFUSAL SIGNATURE: with either bit set, pressing START drives the system state
//   0x133 D1 through 0x60 -> 0x80 (ENABLING) -> 0x60, aborting instead of reaching
//   0xE0 (motor enabled). All 8 attempts in lockedthenstart_2x_kickdownthenstart_2x
//   show this; none reached 0xE0. So a refused start IS observable on the bus.
//   NOTE: there is NO separate "inhibit reason" enum or message channel - the
//   reason is simply which bit is set. The dash message is generated locally from
//   these bits, which is consistent with the cluster also generating the TPMS and
//   ABS/TC warnings itself.
//
// [CONFIRMED] 0x162 D5 bit2 (0x04) = FORK LOCK ENGAGED.
//   0x00 unlocked -> 0x04 locked. Isolated with forklock_3x_5sgap: exactly three
//   contiguous pulses matching the three lock/unlock cycles (49 % of that capture's
//   frames), and bit2 is set in 0 % of all 42 other captures - no false positives.
//   Confirms the EDM's fork lock connector [213] (2-way JAE MX19) is a real sensed
//   input and that its state reaches secondary CAN.
//   NOTE 0x162 D5 is a chassis-status BITFIELD - other bits are in use:
//     bit1 (0x02) appears in kickstand and turn-signal captures (meaning unknown)
//     bit3 (0x08) and bit5 (0x20) appear during ignition transitions
//   Only bit2 is confirmed. Mask it explicitly rather than comparing the whole byte.
//
// [CONFIRMED] 0x162 D6 = kickstand: 0xC8 = DOWN, 0xA8 = UP, 0x28 = UP+motor-ready.
//   Polarity resolved: idle_kickstand_down_freshstartup reads 0xC8 steady (down);
//   kickstand_2xup toggles C8->A8->C8 (up=A8, returns down); 3xup shows 3 A8
//   excursions; ks_up_start_pressed ends at 0x28 (up + motor enabled).
//   (0x181 D4 0xD1<->0xE1 also tracks kickstand but is a shared status byte.)


// ---- 0x163 (355) : BATTERY POWER / ENERGY FLOW (16-step bar) ----------------
BO_ 355 BATTERY_POWER_163: 8 Vector__XXX
 SG_ pack_voltage_163 : 7|16@0+ (0.0625,0) [0|4095] "V" Vector__XXX
 SG_ cooling_pump_163 : 22|1@0+ (1,0) [0|1] "" Vector__XXX
 SG_ temp_mux_sel : 39|8@0+ (1,0) [0|5] "" Vector__XXX
 SG_ temp_mux_raw : 47|8@0+ (1,-40) [-40|215] "degC" Vector__XXX
// [UPDATE 2026-07-27 — ride1 real-timestamp capture + deep review; double-verified.
//  RETRACTS the D2 "16-step battery power bar" (battery_power_level) below.]
//   pack_voltage_163 = (D1:D2) / 16 V  -- a THIRD independent pack-voltage word
//     (agrees with 0x181 pack_voltage and 0x1A7 within ~2.5 V at rest; r 0.91-0.98).
//     WHY THE "BAR" WAS WRONG: legacy captures kept D1 pinned at 0x18, so D2's high
//     nibble alone looked like a 16-step bar ("level 9" = 0x18xx = 393 V rest EMF;
//     "charge levels" = charging voltage rise). ride1's sag drove D1 through
//     {13,14,15,16,17,18} (word 5040->6304 = 315->394 V) with clean byte-carry
//     crossings at the 384 V boundary (0x1810->0x17F0->0x1800) - absurd under a bar.
//   D5/D6 = 6-PHASE TEMPERATURE MUX. temp_mux_sel (D5) cycles 0..5 at 50 Hz (~120 ms
//     revisit). D6 payload, degC = raw-40, is a DIFFERENT channel per selector:
//       sel0 = FAST powertrain temp (inverter/winding-class): ride1 26->81 C through
//              the WOT pull, decaying ~11 C in the 1.1 s coast after (a counter cannot
//              fall). 75mph stand cruise only 68-72 raw. CORPUS MAX 102 C (newcap_5),
//              90 C (newcap_8) - transient spikes carried off by the liquid loop.
//       sel1 = SLOW-MASS temp (motor/coolant-class): ~ambient+2.4 at rest. CORPUS MAX
//              was 42 C on the stand; the STREAMED ROAD RIDES pushed it far higher under
//              sustained load - a clean MONOTONIC heat-soak: 33->42 C (s54 freeway), 42->59 C
//              (s55 record run, +17 C over the old ceiling), 60 C (s57, new streamed max).
//              Still the coolant loop never ran hot enough to trip the 0x1A6 drive fan.
//       sel2-5 = corpus-constant 170/190/170/180 (=130/150/130/140 C) - a plausible
//              limit/derate table, UNEXERCISED (flagged, not decoded).
//     Static regressions across 21 captures: sel1 = 42 + 1.01*ambient (r=0.90),
//     sel0 = 33 + 1.23*ambient (r=0.83) - slope~1, offset~40 confirms the raw-40 scale.
//     *** temp_mux_raw above decodes D6 REGARDLESS of selector - gate on temp_mux_sel
//     before use (sel0/sel1 only). This is the LIVE powertrain temp.
//   *** OPEN THREAD "motor temp on a road ride" RESOLVED HERE [2026-07-29, s54/s55/s57]. ***
//     THIS 0x163 mux IS the motor/inverter temperature: sel1 = motor/coolant-class (33->60 C
//     across the freeway rides), sel0 = inverter/winding-class (streamed rides reconfirmed it,
//     s57 peaked 92 C; legacy corpus max 102 C). It was NEVER on 0x183 D5/D6 - that earlier
//     hypothesis is RETRACTED: 0x183 D5 is the SLOW-MODE / low-SoC power-derate flag (see the
//     0x183 block), and 0x183 D6 is an unexercised spare - neither is a temperature.
//     Absolute-scale/component-ID of the 0x163 mux still wants an IR-thermometer cross-check.
// [CONFIRMED] cooling_pump_163 = 0x163 D3 bit6 (0x40) = MAIN / DRIVE COOLING PUMP ON
//   (wired as a live signal above). 37 (0x25) = off, 73 (0x49) = running. The two states
//   differ in several bits (0x25 vs 0x49); bit6 (0x40) is the clean discriminator (SET in
//   0x49 running, CLEAR in 0x25 off). NOTE: an earlier comment said "bit5 (0x20)" - that
//   was wrong (bit5 is actually SET when OFF, i.e. inverted); validate.py now asserts this.
//   Verified against motor-enabled state (0x133 D1 == 0xE0) across ALL 50 captures:
//   enabled -> 73 without exception; not-enabled -> 37. Only a few single frames
//   differ, at state transitions.
//   Matches the rider's audible report exactly: "the main pump turns on moments
//   after the start button and runs throughout run mode".
//   NOTE it is OFF during charging (0x163 D3 = 37 in every charge capture) - the
//   CHARGER has its own separate circuit, see 0x1A6. The two pumps sound alike but
//   are independently commanded, which is why the rider hears one or the other
//   depending on what the bike is doing.
//   ENCODING CONSISTENCY: the charger's cooling-active flag uses the SAME bit6 in
//   the same 37 (0x25) -> 73 (0x49) pattern on 0x1A6 D3. Two circuits, one convention.
//
// --- SUPERSEDED HISTORICAL DECODE (kept as the research record; do NOT wire) ------
// NAMING: this is BATTERY power, not motor power. It moves during AC charging,
//   which never involves the motor at all, so a motor-centric name would be wrong.
//   Motor-side quantities (phase current, torque command, motor RPM as a control
//   value) are NOT here - the PEU is a PRIMARY-bus module.
// [superseded] 0x163 D2 = BIDIRECTIONAL BATTERY POWER BAR - **quantised**, not raw.
//     level = D2 >> 4     (upper nibble; D2 is ALWAYS a multiple of 16)
//   Every value ever observed across 50 captures: 0x00,0x10,0x20 ... 0xF0 - exactly
//   16 discrete levels, no intermediate values - i.e. it is effectively a 4-BIT
//   field in the upper nibble, with the lower nibble always 0x0.
//   *** IT IS NOT A DASH BAR. *** An earlier note guessed this was the dash
//   power/regen bar; the rider confirms the S2 dash has NO power or regen bar at
//   all. The purpose of the quantisation is UNKNOWN - possibly for the phone app,
//   telematics, or internal energy management. Do not infer a consumer from it.
//     level 9  (0x90 = 144) = ZERO flow
//     levels 10-15 (0xA0..0xF0) = energy INTO the battery  (6 steps)
//     levels 0-8   (0x00..0x80) = energy OUT of the battery (9 steps)
//   COVERS BOTH DIRECTIONS INCLUDING AC CHARGING: during a charge ramp it stepped
//   144 -> 160 -> 176 -> 192 as charge power rose 0 -> ~1.3 kW (cross-referenced
//   against the calibrated 0x1A7 D2 charge power). So it is a true battery-IO
//   indicator, not motor-only - it is the one signal that spans charge AND discharge.
//   CALIBRATION IS NOT LINEAR ACROSS DIRECTIONS - do not extrapolate:
//     charge side: ~0.43 kW per level near zero (3 levels for ~1.3 kW)
//     discharge side: only 9 levels exist, yet the bike makes 63 kW peak. At the
//     charge-side scale that would cap at ~3.9 kW, which is obviously wrong. So the
//     discharge scale is much coarser and/or non-linear (likely a display curve).
//   FOR CONTINUOUS MOTOR LOAD USE 0x161 D2/D4 INSTEAD (see that block) - it is the
//   high-resolution analog. 0x163 remains the only field covering CHARGING too.
//   PRACTICAL USE: treat it as a 4-bit level for LEDs/telemetry - direction and
//   relative intensity are reliable, absolute watts are NOT recoverable from it.
//   (Supersedes two earlier errors: (a) describing it as a CONTINUOUS signed power
//   value - direction and centre were right, continuity was not; and (b) calling it
//   the dash power/regen bar - the dash has no such display.)

// ---- 0x181 (385) : BATTERY / HV STATUS --------------------------------------
BO_ 385 BATTERY_STATUS_181: 8 Vector__XXX
 SG_ pack_voltage : 7|12@0+ (1,0) [0|4095] "V" Vector__XXX
 SG_ pack_current : 11|16@0+ (0.1,-400) [-400|6153.5] "A" Vector__XXX
 SG_ pack_voltage_d5copy : 39|8@0+ (1,256) [256|511] "V" Vector__XXX
// [UPDATE 2026-07-27 — ride1 real-timestamp capture + deep review; double-verified.
//  0x181 IS THE FULL BMS MEASUREMENT FRAME: pack voltage + signed pack current.
//  RETRACTS the "~2.9-3 V/count", batt_flow_181, and the D3 "redundant-voltage" audit
//  below (every legacy observation re-explains as IR-drop on ONE voltage).]
//   pack_voltage = (D1:D2) >> 4 = 1 V/count (12-bit). Rest ~389 V @ 92% SoC; WOT dip
//     to 304 V then recover (ride1 393->304->393). D5 (pack_voltage_d5copy) is a
//     legacy 8-bit COPY = volts-256 (exact or off by <=2) - THIS is what the old
//     "~2.9-3 V/count" was mis-scaling; it is 1 V/count with a 256 offset.
//   pack_current = (D2&0x0F)<<12 | D3<<4 | D4>>4, then I = (raw-4000)/10 A, CHARGE-
//     POSITIVE. Zero-pinned: contactor-open raw=4000 (true 0 A) in 48/80 ignition
//     frames; DC-DC quiescent -0.2..-0.5 A at rest. WOT -189.5 A @ 304 V = 57.6 kW
//     (<63 kW rated). Charging +1.3..+3.9 A at 392-395 V ~ L1 wall power. D4 LOW
//     nibble = validity flag (0 co-occurs with raw==4000 in ~98-100% of open frames).
//   [CHARGE-POSITIVE RE-CONFIRMED 2026-07-28] The charge-positive sign is independently
//     re-confirmed by a sustained external charge (s32-s35: 100% positive pack_i with
//     monotonically rising V/SoC/cells), cross-checked vs UDS RESS 0209 pack current.
//   OHMIC CROSS-CHECK: V = 387.5 - 0.438*I, r=0.9947, rms 1.57 V, n=169 (~4.6 mohm/
//     cell at 96S); slope reproduces cross-capture. Independently cross-validated by
//     the 0x186 Wh meter integral (see 0x186). Continuous pack current/power IS on
//     secondary - RETRACTS the project's "pack current absent" gap.
//   ABSOLUTE 0.1 A/count rests on the wall-wattage match -> pin with an L2 (~5 kW)
//     charge (should read +12..13 A, raw ~4120-4130).
//   (D6 still 0x00 unused. The old counterexamples - "75mph"/manual-spin high current
//    at rest - resolve as unloaded STAND spins, ~-15 A at wheel inertia.)
// --- SUPERSEDED HISTORICAL DECODE (kept as the research record; do NOT wire) ------
// [STRONG] 0x181 D5 = PACK (HV BATTERY) VOLTAGE.
//   FOUND BY SYSTEMATIC TIMELINE CORRELATION (rider's suggestion): correlating every
//   raw byte against every confirmed signal across 6 dynamic captures surfaced
//   0x181 D5 at r=+0.80 vs battery flow - it had been sitting unexplained for the
//   whole project.
//   EVIDENCE IT IS VOLTAGE rather than another power field:
//     1. RESTING VALUE TRACKS SoC MONOTONICALLY across 47 captures:
//          SoC  96% -> 135       SoC  99% -> 136
//          SoC  97% -> 135/136   SoC 100% -> 137  (test.csv 138)
//          SoC  98% -> 135/136
//        Only an open-circuit voltage behaves like this - a power or current field
//        reads the same at rest regardless of SoC.
//     2. SAGS UNDER LOAD: falls to 127 during hard acceleration.
//     3. RISES WHEN CHARGING: 138-139 charging, 139 peak on regen.
//   SCALE: ~2.9-3 V PER COUNT, i.e. a ~400 V pack. RESOLVED BY PHYSICAL MEASUREMENT.
//     The rider measured the HV pack-to-inverter cable at 10 mm OD including
//     insulation. With typical HV wall thickness that implies roughly a 16 mm^2
//     conductor, good for ~110-150 A. At 63 kW peak that demands a HIGH-voltage
//     pack (~400 V, ~160 A). A 136 V pack would need ~460 A, requiring 50-70 mm^2
//     and a 15-20 mm OD - twice what was measured. So raw = volts is WRONG.
//     136 counts x ~2.9 V = ~394 V, which at ~96 series cells is 4.1 V/cell at full
//     charge - exactly as sensible as the 33s arithmetic below.
//   *** RETRACTED: "raw = volts, ~136 V pack, ~33s". The cell-voltage arithmetic
//     that supported it is WORTHLESS AS EVIDENCE because it works equally well for
//     BOTH hypotheses (33 cells at 1 V/count and 96 cells at ~2.9 V/count both give
//     ~4.1 V/cell). It only looked convincing because the alternative was never
//     checked. Lesson: internal consistency of a scaling assumption is not evidence
//     unless the competing assumption fails it.
//   *** ALSO RETRACTED: the "no DC fast charging implies a sub-threshold pack"
//     argument. At ~400 V the S2 is comfortably above any DCFC minimum, so the
//     omission is a cost / packaging / thermal decision, not physics.
//   REMAINING UNCERTAINTY: the exact counts-per-volt (2.9? 3.0?) is not pinned - the
//     cable gauge fixes the ORDER OF MAGNITUDE, not the precise scale.
//   DEFINITIVE TEST: a UDS read of the RESS returning pack voltage in known units.
// [STRONG] 0x181 D2:D3 (16-bit) = another battery flow / current-like channel.
//   r = +0.874 vs battery flow, -0.732 vs motor load. Rest 33017, charging 45307,
//   ride 241..61681. Bidirectional about rest with discharge swinging ~3x further
//   than regen, consistent with drive-vs-regen asymmetry. Resolution (30 distinct
//   values in a ride) is comparable to existing flow fields, not better - so it is
//   NOT the "true continuous" battery power still being sought.
// FULL FRAME MAP for 0x181 (audited byte by byte):
//   D1 = 23-24, essentially constant. Header / status. No information yet.
//   D2 = battery-flow-like, QUANTISED to multiples of 16 (0..240). r=+0.87 vs
//        battery flow, -0.73 vs motor load. Another stepped flow copy, not finer.
//   D3 = REDUNDANT PACK-VOLTAGE MEASUREMENT (hypothesis, well supported).
//        r=+0.977 vs D5, same 13 distinct values, band 236-252, same direction
//        (falls under load, rises charging), and sags by nearly the same ABSOLUTE
//        amount as D5 (6 vs 7 counts) - i.e. same scale.
//        KEY EVIDENCE: D3 - D5 is tightly clustered at 113 but NOT constant - it
//        spans 111..115 (113 = 51 %, 112 = 21 %, 114 = 17 %, 111 = 11 %). If D3 were
//        derived arithmetically from D5 the difference would be EXACTLY fixed. The
//        +/-2 count jitter means these are TWO INDEPENDENT MEASUREMENTS of the same
//        quantity, sharing a scale but using a different encoding offset.
//        WHY A SYSTEM WOULD DO THIS: safety-critical HV packs measure voltage on two
//        separate sense paths and cross-check them. Deliberately DIFFERENT offsets
//        mean a stuck bit or failed ADC cannot produce two matching plausible values
//        - any disagreement beyond a threshold flags a sensor fault. The +/-2 counts
//        is the expected noise between two ADC chains.
//        RULED OUT: D3 as MINIMUM CELL VOLTAGE. A weakest-cell reading must sag
//        proportionally HARDER than the pack under load; D3 sags 2.6 % against the
//        pack's 5.2 %, i.e. LESS. Not a cell-level metric.
//   D4 = shared STATUS byte (0xD1<->0xE1 tracks kickstand, but it also moves during
//        signal tests). Do NOT pair D3:D4 as a 16-bit value - although that pairing
//        correlates r=+0.985 with pack voltage and shows 77 distinct values, the
//        SPREAD WITHIN a single D5 step (up to 496) exceeds the step size (~238),
//        i.e. the extra "resolution" is D4's status bits leaking in, not real
//        precision. A tempting false lead.
//   D5 = PACK VOLTAGE (see above).
//   D6 = always 0x00 in every capture. Unused.
//   WHAT ITS CONSUMERS NEED (reasoning from the three secondary-bus receivers -
//   the IM/dash, the TCU/app, and the DLC - since the RESS itself is on primary,
//   everything gatewayed here serves a DISPLAY, an APP or a DIAGNOSTIC, never a
//   control loop):
//     satisfied here  -> pack voltage (the dash Diagnostics page shows voltages),
//                        battery flow (app power display), redundancy cross-check
//     satisfied elsewhere -> SoC (0x185), charge state (0x1A7), temps (0x183)
//     STILL UNLOCATED, and worth hunting because a dash/app plausibly needs them:
//       * BATTERY WARNING / FAULT flags (over-voltage, under-voltage, over-temp).
//         Likely a bitfield; look for a byte that is 0 in all healthy captures.
//       * STATE OF HEALTH (SoH). Would be near-constant across every capture and
//         change only over months - effectively invisible in this dataset. A byte
//         sitting at a fixed high value (e.g. ~100, ~200, ~255) is a candidate.
//       * AVAILABLE POWER LIMIT (discharge/charge ceilings the BMS computes from
//         SoC and temperature). Would move with temperature and SoC but NOT with
//         instantaneous load - a distinctive signature worth testing when the pack
//         is cold vs hot.
//       * TIME-TO-FULL while charging - would count DOWN during a charge session.
//   CONCLUSION: this frame contains NO continuous absolute power value. Its flow
//   channels (D2) are stepped like all the others.


// ---- 0x260 (608) : mode mirror ----------------------------------------------
BO_ 608 MODE_MIRROR_260: 8 Vector__XXX
 SG_ ride_mode_mirror : 7|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
 SG_ ride_mode_mirror_d2 : 15|8@0+ (1,0) [0|255] "" Vector__XXX
// [CONFIRMED] 0x260 D1 mirrors 0x134 D1 ride-mode enum exactly
//   (22->88->99->33->44->11->22 through the mode cycle).
// [CONFIRMED] 0x260 D2 high-nibble = mode id too (20/10/40/30/80/90 for
//   Sport/Street/Eco/Rain/CustA/CustB); low nibble 0. See the 0x134 mode table.


// ---- 0x562 (1378) : ODOMETER ------------------------------------------------
BO_ 1378 ODOMETER_562: 8 IM
